<p>Switch to the <strong>DFA</strong> tab to see the determinized version — each DFA state is a set of NFA states, computed by subset construction. The legend below the graph shows the mapping.</p>
<h2 id="subset-construction">Subset Construction</h2>
<p>The NFA simulates nondeterminism by tracking <em>all possible states simultaneously</em>. Subset construction makes this explicit: each set of NFA states becomes a single DFA state.</p>
<p>The algorithm is a BFS:</p>
<ol>
<li>Start with the ε-closure of the NFA&rsquo;s initial state. That&rsquo;s your first DFA state.</li>
<li>For each DFA state, for each symbol, compute: move (follow symbol transitions), then ε-closure. The resulting set is the next DFA state.</li>
<li>Repeat until no new DFA states are discovered.</li>
<li>A DFA state is accepting if it contains <em>any</em> NFA accepting state.</li>
</ol>
<p>The result: a machine that makes exactly one transition per input character. No branching. No backtracking. O(n) matching, guaranteed.</p>
<h2 id="what-regex-cant-do">What Regex Can&rsquo;t Do</h2>
<p>Finite states = finite memory = no unbounded counting.</p>
<p>A DFA can&rsquo;t match &ldquo;n <code>a</code>s followed by n <code>b</code>s&rdquo; (like <code>aaabbb</code>). It would need to <em>remember how many</em> <code>a</code>s it saw, but it has no counter — just a fixed set of states. By the Pumping Lemma, any sufficiently long string accepted by a finite automaton contains a pumpable substring, which breaks the equal-count requirement.</p>
<p>This is why you can&rsquo;t match balanced parentheses, valid HTML nesting, or palindromes with regex. The machine would need infinitely many states. For that, you need a pushdown automaton (context-free) or a Turing machine.</p>
<p>The limitation is exactly why regex is fast. Finite states means finite work per character. No stack, no tape, no backtracking — just a lookup table.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>For the formal treatment: Sipser, &ldquo;Introduction to the Theory of Computation,&rdquo; Chapter 1.</em></p>
